The throwable hierarchy — checked versus unchecked.

Everything you can throw or catch in Java is an object, and every one of them descends from a single root class: throwable. Its shape is not decoration. The branches of this hierarchy encode who is responsible for an error — and that determines whether the compiler forces you to deal with it.

There are three tiers under throwable. At the top, throwable splits into two big branches: error, and exception. Error signals a failure in the java virtual machine itself — out of memory, or stack overflow. These are not meant to be caught; there's nothing your code can do to recover, so you let them stop the program. Exception is the other branch — application-level problems, the ones your code throws and handles. And exception itself splits into two kinds. That split is the most important distinction in the whole topic.

The line runs through exception. Runtime-exception, and its subclasses, are unchecked. Every other exception is checked. And the difference is entirely about what the compiler demands.

A checked exception — like input-output-exception, or sequel-exception — represents a failure outside your control, but foreseeable. The disk is full; the network dropped. The compiler forces you to acknowledge it: either you catch it, or you declare "throws that exception" on your method. Skip both, and the code won't compile.

An unchecked exception — like null-pointer-exception, illegal-argument-exception, or index-out-of-bounds-exception — represents a programming bug. A contract you violated. The compiler says nothing about these; they can be thrown anywhere, and you're expected to fix the bug, not catch it. So a read method that calls files-dot-read-string must declare "throws input-output-exception", because that call is checked. But a method that calls get with an index of ninety-nine can throw index-out-of-bounds with no declaration at all — because it's unchecked.

Here's how to read the split as intent. It's really a design conversation, baked into the type system, asking: is this a condition a caller could reasonably anticipate and recover from — or is it a broken assumption in the code? If it's recoverable and external, it's checked. "The file might not be there" is a real possibility a caller must plan for, and the compiler makes them plan. If it's a bug in the program, it's unchecked. "I dereferenced null", or "I passed a negative size" — that isn't recovered at runtime, it's fixed in the source. And forcing a try-catch around every possible bug would drown the real logic in noise.

So what are the practical consequences? This tier decides your daily habits. You catch checked exceptions, because you must, and because you often can do something useful — retry, fall back, report. You generally don't catch unchecked ones — you let them surface as the loud stack trace from section one, the one that points you straight at the bug to fix. And you never catch error. Later, when you design your own exceptions in section four, this same choice returns: extend runtime-exception for a caller-bug, and extend exception for a recoverable condition you want callers to handle deliberately.
